Building a projection mapping app for modern smartphones requires bridging a highly responsive touch interface with raw GPU rendering power. Because modern phones can easily handle 4K video decoding, we can build a modular architecture that scales from running entirely on the phone to acting as a remote control for dedicated projection hardware.Here is a comprehensive blueprint for building the application.System Architecture & Tech StackTo keep the application cross-platform and highly performant, we must strictly decouple the user interface from the rendering engine.The Control Dashboard (Frontend): Build the UI using React and Tailwind CSS. This ensures a clean, responsive layout for managing media assets, layers, and project states.The Touch Mapping Layer: Use interactive SVG overlays to handle the touch mechanics. SVGs are perfect for calculating the mathematical precision of bezier curves, masks, and corner-pinning without interfering with the video rendering thread.The Rendering Engine: Use the new WebGPU API. WebGPU is the successor to WebGL, providing native-like access to the device's graphics hardware. It allows for advanced compute shaders and drastically improved parallel processing, which is critical for handling multiple warped video layers without dropping frames.   Remote Hardware Mode (Pro Feature): While the app can project directly via a USB-C to HDMI adapter, complex installations require dedicated hardware. We can build a companion Flask API packaged in a Docker container meant to run on a Raspberry Pi cluster or a home server. The smartphone app connects over Wi-Fi via WebSockets, transforming the phone into a lightweight remote control for the heavy rendering cluster.The Real-Time Rendering PipelineTo ensure 60fps playback and prevent the phone from thermally throttling, every video frame must bypass the CPU entirely.1.Hardware Decoding:Bypassing the CPU.The smartphone's native hardware decoder reads the media file (H.264/HEVC) and pushes the raw frame directly into the GPU's Video RAM.2.Vertex Warping:The React UI sends the dragged SVG corner coordinates to the WebGPU vertex shader. A densely subdivided mesh grid is instantly recalculated to match the physical surface.3.Fragment Processing:The fragment shader applies the video texture to the warped mesh, running any real-time effects (color correction, edge blending, or masking) natively on the GPU.4.Display Output:The final processed frame is pushed out through the phone's physical display output directly to the connected projector.Phased Development RoadmapBuilding this requires a staged approach, securing the core rendering mechanics on the device before expanding to the remote hardware network.The Rendering Core & MVPPhase 1 (Months 1-2)Establish the WebGPU canvas and the hardware-accelerated video texture pipeline.Build the React/Tailwind UI and the math for the bilinear interpolation mesh (translating SVG touch coordinates to the GPU).Result: A functional, single-layer projection app that outputs via HDMI.Advanced Masking & Multi-LayeringPhase 2 (Months 3-4)Introduce bezier curve mapping for curved architectural surfaces.Add support for multiple simultaneous video layers and custom alpha-channel masks.Implement basic audio-reactive shaders utilizing the phone's microphone.The Remote Hardware BridgePhase 3 (Months 5-6)Develop the Python/Flask backend and Dockerize the rendering engine.Build the WebSocket communication layer so the React app can sync state with the remote Raspberry Pi cluster in real-time.Result: The app can now seamlessly toggle between local projection and remote hardware control.Beta Testing & LaunchPhase 4 (Month 7)Optimize memory management to prevent memory leaks and thermal throttling during extended use.Finalize UI polishing, onboarding tutorials, and launch on app stores.Feature Tiering & Market PlacementTo compete with existing mobile mapping tools like PicoMap and ProjectX, the business model should lower the barrier to entry for beginners while charging for professional hardware scalability.   FeatureBasic Tier (Free)Pro Tier (One-Time or Subscription)Video LayersSingle layer projectionUnlimited layers & complex groupingMasking4-corner pin, basic shapesBezier curves, custom drawn SVG masksOutput MethodDirect USB-C / HDMI onlyRemote Docker/Flask node controlVisual EffectsStandard color correctionAudio-reactive shaders, edge blending